#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055942 :tutorial/content "The **Cartesian coordinate system** (or **Cartesian plane**) is a grid that we can use to understand where certain objects lie, similar to a map. This grid looks as follows:\n\n![](images/fd/fd702a53ff1127a2432cbdd9a3c7057a08c73ac7a086454c066fa2c96bea7974.png)\n\nIt consists of a horizontal number line called the **$x$-axis** and a vertical number line called the **$y$-axis**.\n\nThe **origin** is the location where the $x$ and $y$ axes meet and is given the symbol $O.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dacc8373-6186-517b-bba8-ec1729732e4d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1431 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055943 :tutorial/content "Every location on the Cartesian plane is called a **point**. Every point is identified using an $x$**-coordinate** and a $y$**-coordinate**. Together, points are expressed using an **ordered pair** of **coordinates** $(x,y).$\n\nFor example, we can mark (or **plot**) the point $A$ with coordinates $({\\color{red}{3}},{\\color{blue}{2}})$ by starting from $O$ and then traveling $\\color{red}3$ units towards the right, followed $\\color{blue}2$ units up.\n\n![](images/c1/c1ef61dedbbcc6eedcd125c2c62cdfbaa4d9b9a12f10181714b51c060a059fd3.png)\n\nWe say that\n\n- the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $3$,\n\n- the $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $2$,\n\n- the $xy$-coordinates (or just \"the coordinates\") of $A$ are $(3,2).$\n\nThe letter $A$ is just a label for our point. It's usually a good idea to label points!" :tutorial/id #uuid "9e7847c5-757a-5cb8-b947-9c7d806b5dc5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1432 :tutorial/title "Coordinates and Ordered Pairs"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055957 :tutorial/content "Remember that a coordinate plane is made by two number lines that meet at $0.$ Since these are number lines, we can extend both to include negative numbers.\n\n![](images/f1/f1eeb7f3bc0ab8b39e9dc7127d76a32f1dd130d380748fedc6e11b967f3878ed.png)\n\nWe plot an ordered pair $({\\color{red}x},{\\color{blue}y})$ similar to before, but if the number is negative, we reverse the direction. So, starting at the origin:\n\n- if the first number is positive, move that number of places *right* along the $\\color{red}x$-axis. If its negative, move *left*.\n\n- if the second number is positive, move that number of places *up* along the $\\color{blue}y$-axis. If its negative, move *down*.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the coordinates of the point $Q$ above.\n\nTo find the coordinates of $Q,$ we start at the origin, move $\\color{red}3$ units *left*, and then $\\color{blue}2$ units *down*. \n\n![](images/b4/b4f58f0c1ad6dc2936ed1b24c844fa82c1b1d5ec10c80e14c6a43f6f4eaf5544.png)\n\nSince we move left, the $\\color{red}x$-coordinate is negative, and since we move down, the $\\color{blue}y$-coordinate is negative.\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $Q$ are $({\\color{red}{-3}},{\\color{blue}{-2}}).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7383452c-9470-5f05-b206-dffbb84808dd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2092 :tutorial/title "Extending the Coordinate Plane to Negative Numbers"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055958 :tutorial/content "The axes split the coordinate plane into four parts, called **quadrants**.\n\n![](images/93/938125e855fcb96f743c01f2a00d289ef13e0601772bab56cf0f255e9adf4dcd.png)\n\nGoing counter-clockwise, it's common to call\n\n- the top-right quadrant the first quadrant (I),\n\n- the top-left is the second quadrant (II),\n\n- the bottom-left is the third quadrant (III),\n\n- and and bottom-right is the fourth quadrant (IV).\n\nTo remember the order of the quadrants, it's helpful to think about writing the letter \"C\" in the graph:\n\n![](images/1e/1ef91f2bc25ea8b887ec449d2dafe2eaf6a157733e7bc50e4480280df8190728.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "8a1e27dd-9f87-5adf-b0c6-f0f6cd7e2aac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2097 :tutorial/title "Quadrants in the Coordinate Plane"}]}
